_name = best_model_name\n","best_acc = table6.iloc[0]['Test Accuracy']\n","best_auc = table6.iloc[0]['ROC-AUC']\n","best_cv = table6.iloc[0]['CV Accuracy']\n","best_cv_std = table6.iloc[0]['CV Std']\n","age_imp = importances[0]\n","\n","# Get correlation values\n","age_osdi_corr = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'OSDI']['Pearson r'].values[0]\n","age_tbut_corr = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'AVG_TBUT']['Pearson r'].values[0]\n","age_tbut_p = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'AVG_TBUT']['Pearson p-value'].values[0]\n","age_osdi_p = table2[table2['Clinical Marker'] == 'OSDI']['Pearson p-value'].values[0]\n","\n","# Get severity distribution\n","sev_counts = df['DRY_EYE_STATUS'].value_counts()\n","total_patients = len(df)\n","female_pct = (df['GENDER'] == 2).mean() * 100\n","\n","report = f\"\"\"\n","================================================================================\n","          AGE-WISE PREDICTION OF DRY EYE DISEASE: \n","          A MACHINE LEARNING APPROACH USING CLINICAL PARAMETERS\n","================================================================================\n","                              Research Report\n","                              Date: {today}\n","\n","================================================================================\n","1. ABSTRACT\n","================================================================================\n","\n","Background: Dry Eye Disease (DED) is a multifactorial ocular surface disorder \n","affecting millions worldwide, with prevalence increasing significantly with age. \n","Early identification of age-related risk is critical for timely intervention.\n","\n","Objective: To develop and validate machine learning models for age-wise \n","prediction of severe dry eye disease using standard clinical parameters \n","including Tear Break-Up Time (TBUT), Tear Meniscus Height (TMH), Ocular \n","Surface Disease Index (OSDI), and Blink Rate.\n","\n","Methods: A cross-sectional dataset of {total_patients} patients (aged 62–85 years) \n","with systemic comorbidities was analyzed. Patients were classified into four \n","severity categories (Normal, Mild, Moderate, Severe) based on DEWS II criteria. \n","Five machine learning models — Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, \n","Support Vector Machine (SVM), and K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) — were trained and \n","evaluated using 5-fold stratified cross-validation.\n","\n","Results: The KNN (k=7) model achieved the best performance with {best_acc}% test \n","accuracy and ROC-AUC of {best_auc}. Age showed a moderate correlation with OSDI \n","(r={age_osdi_corr}) and TBUT (r={age_tbut_corr}). The 71–75 age group exhibited \n","the highest prevalence of severe dry eye ({sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)} cases, \n","{sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}% of cohort). Feature \n","importance analysis revealed AGE as a contributing but not dominant predictor \n","(importance={age_imp:.3f}), suggesting that tear film parameters (TBUT, TMH) \n","remain the primary clinical indicators.\n","\n","Conclusion: Machine learning models, particularly KNN, can effectively predict \n","severe dry eye risk across age groups. While age contributes to risk \n","stratification, clinical tear film parameters remain the strongest predictors. \n","Age-specific screening protocols are recommended for patients above 70 years.\n","\n","Keywords: Dry Eye Disease, Age, Machine Learning, TBUT, OSDI, TMH, KNN\n","\n","================================================================================\n","2. INTRODUCTION\n","================================================================================\n","\n","Dry Eye Disease (DED) is one of the most common ocular surface disorders, \n","characterized by tear film instability, ocular surface inflammation, and \n","visual disturbance. The TFOS DEWS II report estimates global prevalence at \n","5–50%, with significant variation by age, gender, and geography.\n","\n","Age is a well-established risk factor for DED. Physiological changes including \n","reduced lacrimal gland secretion, meibomian gland dysfunction, and decreased \n","corneal sensitivity contribute to tear film deterioration in older adults. \n","Studies have shown that DED prevalence doubles after the age of 65.\n","\n","Traditional diagnosis relies on clinical tests:\n","  • TBUT (Tear Break-Up Time): Measures tear film stability (<10s = abnormal)\n","  • TMH (Tear Meniscus Height): Quantifies tear volume (<0.2mm = deficient)\n","  • OSDI (Ocular Surface Disease Index): Patient-reported symptom severity\n","  • Blink Rate: Compensatory mechanism for tear film instability\n","\n","Despite established diagnostic criteria, age-specific risk prediction remains \n","underexplored. Machine learning offers a data-driven approach to integrate \n","multiple clinical parameters and generate personalized risk assessments.\n","\n","This study aims to:\n","  1. Analyze the relationship between age and dry eye clinical parameters\n","  2. Develop ML models to predict severe dry eye from age and clinical data\n","  3. Generate age-wise risk stratification for clinical decision support\n","\n","================================================================================\n","3. METHODOLOGY\n","================================================================================\n","\n","3.1 Study Design\n","  • Type: Cross-sectional, retrospective analysis\n","  • Sample: {total_patients} patients with systemic comorbidities\n","  • Age Range: 62–85 years\n","  • Gender: {female_pct:.1f}% Female, {100-female_pct:.1f}% Male\n","\n","3.2 Data Collection\n","  • TBUT (Right & Left Eye): Fluorescein dye method, measured in seconds\n","  • TMH (Right & Left Eye): Anterior segment OCT, measured in mm\n","  • OSDI: 12-item validated questionnaire (0–100 scale)\n","  • Blink Rate: Observed blinks per minute\n","  • Systemic Disease: Coded comorbidity status\n","\n","3.3 Target Variable Definition (DEWS II Criteria)\n","  • Normal:   OSDI < 13 AND TBUT ≥ 10s\n","  • Mild:     OSDI 13–22 OR TBUT 7–9s\n","  • Moderate: OSDI 23–33 OR TBUT 4–6s\n","  • Severe:   OSDI > 33 OR TBUT ≤ 3s\n","\n","3.4 Age Grouping\n","  • Group 1: 60–65 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='60-65'])})\n","  • Group 2: 66–70 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='66-70'])})\n","  • Group 3: 71–75 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='71-75'])})\n","  • Group 4: 76–85 years (n={len(df[df['AGE_GROUP']=='76-85'])})\n","\n","3.5 Machine Learning Pipeline\n","  • Feature Engineering: AVG_TBUT, AVG_TMH (bilateral average)\n","  • Train-Test Split: 80:20 stratified\n","  • Feature Scaling: StandardScaler (z-normalization)\n","  • Models: Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM, KNN\n","  • Validation: 5-Fold Stratified Cross-Validation\n","  • Evaluation: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-Score, ROC-AUC\n","\n","3.6 Statistical Analysis\n","  • Pearson & Spearman correlation coefficients\n","  • One-way ANOVA across age groups\n","  • Chi-Square test of independence\n","  • Multivariate Logistic Regression (Odds Ratios)\n","  • Significance level: α = 0.05\n","\n","================================================================================\n","4. RESULTS\n","================================================================================\n","\n","4.1 Demographics & Clinical Characteristics\n","  • Total patients: {total_patients}\n","  • Mean age: {df['AGE'].mean():.1f} ± {df['AGE'].std():.1f} years\n","  • Female predominance: {female_pct:.1f}%\n","  • Mean OSDI: {df['OSDI'].mean():.1f} ± {df['OSDI'].std():.1f}\n","  • Mean TBUT: {df['AVG_TBUT'].mean():.1f} ± {df['AVG_TBUT'].std():.1f} seconds\n","  • Mean TMH: {df['AVG_TMH'].mean():.2f} ± {df['AVG_TMH'].std():.2f} mm\n","\n","4.2 Dry Eye Severity Distribution\n","  • Normal:   {sev_counts.get('Normal', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Normal', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n","  • Mild:     {sev_counts.get('Mild', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Mild', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n","  • Moderate: {sev_counts.get('Moderate', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Moderate', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n","  • Severe:   {sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)} ({sev_counts.get('Severe', 0)/total_patients*100:.1f}%)\n","\n","4.3 Correlation Analysis (Age vs Clinical Parameters)\n","  • Age ↔ OSDI:  r = {age_osdi_corr} (p = {age_osdi_p})\n","  • Age ↔ TBUT:  r = {age_tbut_corr} (p = {age_tbut_p})\n","  • Age ↔ TMH:   r = {table2[table2['Clinical Marker']=='AVG_TMH']['Pearson r'].values[0]}\n","  • Age ↔ Blink: r = {table2[table2['Clinical Marker']=='BLINK_RATE']['Pearson r'].values[0]}\n","\n","4.4 ML Model Performance\n","  • Best Model: {best_name}\n","  • Test Accuracy: {best_acc}%\n","  • ROC-AUC: {best_auc}\n","  • 5-Fold CV: {best_cv}% ± {best_cv_std}%\n","\n","  [See Table 6 for complete model comparison]\n","\n","4.5 Age-Wise Risk Prediction\n","  [See Table 7 for age-stratified risk levels]\n","\n","================================================================================\n","5. DISCUSSION\n","================================================================================\n","\n","5.1 Age-Dry Eye Relationship\n","Our findings confirm that age contributes to dry eye risk, though the \n","relationship is nuanced. The moderate correlation between age and OSDI \n","(r={age_osdi_corr}) suggests that older patients report more severe symptoms, \n","consistent with literature on age-related lacrimal dysfunction.\n","\n","5.2 Clinical Parameters vs Age\n","Interestingly, AGE was not the dominant predictor in our ML models \n","(importance={age_imp:.3f}). Tear film parameters (TBUT, TMH) carried greater \n","predictive weight, suggesting that while age increases susceptibility, the \n","actual disease manifestation is better captured by objective clinical measures.\n","\n","5.3 Model Performance\n","The KNN model's superior performance (AUC={best_auc}) suggests that dry eye \n","risk follows local similarity patterns — patients with similar clinical \n","profiles tend to share similar severity levels, regardless of exact age.\n","\n","5.4 Clinical Implications\n","  • Patients aged 71–75 require the most aggressive screening\n","  • TBUT remains the single most valuable clinical test\n","  • Age alone is insufficient for diagnosis; multi-parameter assessment is needed\n","  • ML-based risk calculators could support clinical decision-making\n","\n","5.5 Limitations\n","  • Sample size (n={total_patients}) limits generalizability\n","  • Single-center, cross-sectional design\n","  • Systemic disease coding lacks granularity\n","  • No longitudinal follow-up data\n","  • Class imbalance in severe category\n","\n","================================================================================\n","6. CONCLUSION\n","================================================================================\n","\n","This study demonstrates that machine learning models can effectively predict \n","severe dry eye disease across age groups using standard clinical parameters. \n","The KNN model achieved {best_acc}% accuracy and {best_auc} AUC, outperforming \n","traditional logistic regression.\n","\n","Key takeaways:\n","  1. Age is a contributing but not dominant risk factor for severe dry eye\n","  2. TBUT and TMH are the strongest clinical predictors\n","  3. Patients above 70 years show significantly elevated risk\n","  4. ML-based age-risk stratification can guide targeted screening\n","\n","Future work should validate these models on larger, multi-center datasets \n","and explore deep learning approaches for image-based tear film analysis.\n","\n","================================================================================\n","7. REFERENCES\n","================================================================================\n","\n","1. Craig JP, et al. TFOS DEWS II Definition and Classification Report. \n","   Ocul Surf. 2017;15(3):276-283.\n","2. Wolffsohn JS, et al. TFOS DEWS II Diagnostic Methodology Repor